1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,774
What are dynamic workflows, and why would
you use them?

2
00:00:05,050 --> 00:00:08,707
The tests passed. That's a good start, but
what's still

3
00:00:08,707 --> 00:00:12,440
untested? Dynamic workflows bring commands,
AI

4
00:00:12,440 --> 00:00:15,562
agents, and review checkpoints into one
reusable

5
00:00:15,945 --> 00:00:16,629
process.

6
00:00:17,700 --> 00:00:20,974
Here are a few examples of where dynamic
workflows can help.

7
00:00:21,885 --> 00:00:25,961
Prepare a release with tests and a list of
what's untested. Review

8
00:00:25,961 --> 00:00:29,692
a pull request with the checks and findings
in one place.

9
00:00:29,796 --> 00:00:33,123
Investigate a failed build by gathering
logs and possible next

10
00:00:33,123 --> 00:00:36,625
steps. Let's look at a simple example with
a Python book

11
00:00:36,628 --> 00:00:37,387
app.

12
00:00:38,350 --> 00:00:42,252
Here's the plan. Run the tests first. Then,
take a look at the

13
00:00:42,636 --> 00:00:46,443
results. Find the gaps with an AI agent.
We'll save the

14
00:00:46,560 --> 00:00:50,557
report so we can decide what needs work.
This is our own workflow,

15
00:00:50,560 --> 00:00:54,214
not a built-in command. Let's create book
release check.

16
00:00:55,550 --> 00:00:59,700
First, tell Copilot what the workflow
should do. We give it a name,

17
00:00:59,931 --> 00:01:03,967
describe the checks and review checkpoint,
and ask it to save a reusable

18
00:01:04,007 --> 00:01:07,700
project extension. Creating it doesn't run
it.

19
00:01:08,650 --> 00:01:12,076
Copilot turns that request into code and
registers the

20
00:01:12,460 --> 00:01:16,495
workflow. The saved extension holds the
steps, including our pause for

21
00:01:16,840 --> 00:01:20,190
review. Now book release check is ready to
run.

22
00:01:21,150 --> 00:01:25,226
Let's ask Copilot to run book release
check. This starts the workflow

23
00:01:25,226 --> 00:01:28,881
we just created, beginning with the tests
and help check.

24
00:01:29,850 --> 00:01:33,733
The checks are done. The workflow's paused
here, so we can look at the

25
00:01:33,812 --> 00:01:35,867
results before going any further.

26
00:01:36,850 --> 00:01:40,888
All five tests passed, and the help command
works. But does

27
00:01:40,888 --> 00:01:44,695
it handle damaged data? And can we find
authors regardless of

28
00:01:44,736 --> 00:01:47,819
capitalization? Let's have the agent check.

29
00:01:48,783 --> 00:01:52,781
Type slash workflows, pick our paused
release check, and press

30
00:01:52,783 --> 00:01:55,752
Enter. Now we can see what's happened so
far.

31
00:01:56,717 --> 00:02:00,717
Press R to keep going. We don't need to
rerun those checks; their

32
00:02:00,717 --> 00:02:04,679
results are saved. The agent now looks
through the code and tests for

33
00:02:04,679 --> 00:02:05,667
gaps.

34
00:02:06,617 --> 00:02:10,652
That's finished. Let's open the report and
see what needs attention.

35
00:02:11,617 --> 00:02:15,500
The report gives us two things to test
next: handling a damaged

36
00:02:15,502 --> 00:02:18,319
data file and finding authors regardless of

37
00:02:18,360 --> 00:02:22,052
capitalization. So, before we ship, we know
where to spend our

38
00:02:22,312 --> 00:02:22,919
time.

39
00:02:23,883 --> 00:02:27,896
If you keep doing the same checks by hand,
this is worth trying.

40
00:02:27,896 --> 00:02:31,883
The QR code links to the guide. Use slash
workflows to keep

41
00:02:31,883 --> 00:02:35,462
track of your runs. Give dynamic workflows
a try.
